<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pablo Morillo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pablo Morillo was a Spanish general who led the large royalist expedition sent in 1815 to reconquer Venezuela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>. He besieged Cartagena and starved it into surrender in 1815, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> in 1816 and carried out harsh reprisals against patriot leaders. In 1820 he signed an armistice with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pablo-morillo.jpg" alt="Pablo Morillo" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horace Vernet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pablo-morillo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Morillo was born to a peasant family in Fuentesecas and joined the Spanish Royal Marine Corps at 16. He fought at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> in 1793, was taken prisoner when the San Isidro was captured at the Battle of Cape St. Vincent in 1797, and was wounded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trafalgar">Battle of Trafalgar</a> in 1805. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> broke out he joined the army, fought at the Battle of Bailen, and won fame at the Battle of Puente Sanpayo, where his troops forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-ney">Marshal Ney</a>'s French force to retreat and leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. He rose to field marshal during the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 14 August 1814 Ferdinand VII gave him command of an expeditionary army, publicly bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> but secretly redirected to Venezuela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>. It sailed from Cádiz on 17 February 1815 with 18 warships and 42 troopships carrying some 10,400 troops, most of them Peninsular veterans; the flagship San Pedro Alcántara exploded and sank on 25 April with a thousand crew. His 105-day siege of Cartagena earned him the title Count of Cartagena. In Santa Fe he revoked an amnesty granted by Miguel de la Torre, and more than a hundred republican officials were executed, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camilo-torres-tenorio">Camilo Torres Tenorio</a>, Francisco Jose de Caldas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-tadeo-lozano">Jorge Tadeo Lozano</a>.</p><p>Back in Venezuela he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> to a stalemate and defeated him at the Third Battle of La Puerta in 1818, where he was wounded, earning the title Marquess of La Puerta. After the uprising of Rafael del Riego on 1 January 1820 stopped the reinforcements assembling at Cádiz, Morillo was ordered to have the colonies obey the Constitution of 1812. He met Bolívar at Santa Ana on 26 November 1820 and signed a six-month armistice and a second treaty on the conduct of the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> he became Captain General of New Castile, supported the constitution during the Liberal Triennium, prevented a coup against it in 1822, and was defeated in the north in 1823 by the French invasion under the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>. When Ferdinand VII restored absolute rule he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, later returning to serve in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlist-wars">Carlist Wars</a>. He died at Barèges on 27 July 1837. His contemporaries knew him as El Pacificador.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pablo Morillo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pablo Morillo's</b> royalist army retook Bogotá in 1816, ending the first period of independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-and-independent-state-of-cundinamarca">Free and Independent State of Cundinamarca</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pablo Morillo's</b> royalist expedition besieged Cartagena in 1815, starving it into surrender, and took Bogotá in May 1816.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-provinces-of-new-granada">United Provinces of New Granada</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pablo Morillo</b> reconquered the territory in 1816, but Simón Bolívar's victory at Boyacá in 1819 ended Spanish rule and led to the creation of Gran Colombia.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-new-granada">Viceroyalty of New Granada</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fuentesecas, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Barèges, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.63,-5.47&amp;year=1815" aria-label="Fuentesecas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pablo Morillo"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4651 4004h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4651 4004h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.63,-5.47&amp;year=1815">What was here in 1815? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
